{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.FourDilogFirstOrder","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.FourDilogFirstOrder","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.e2_bar_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.e2_bar_even","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L95-L99","doc":"**`e₂` is bar-EVEN.** Under `aᵢ ↦ −aᵢ` the quadratic (pairwise) part is invariant — matching\nthe bar-even gap `h²` of the #4158 decomposition. So the octahedral 4-`Φ_q` holonomy splits the\n`−9.21%` as bar-odd linear ⊕ bar-even quadratic, the same parities as the Magnus route. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.e1_bar_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.e1_bar_odd","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L90-L93","doc":"**`e₁` is bar-ODD.** Under `aᵢ ↦ −aᵢ` the linear part flips sign — matching the bar-odd\nimpurity `−t` of the #4158 decomposition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.e2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.e2","kind":"def","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L87-L88","doc":"The **quadratic order** `e₂` (the pairwise tetrahedron products — the Magnus / cross terms). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.e1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.e1","kind":"def","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L84-L85","doc":"The **linear order** `e₁` of the octahedral expansion (the first-order tetrahedron sum). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_linear_part","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_linear_part","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L64-L72","doc":"**The missing part is second-order-and-up in the missing tetrahedra together with their\nfirst order.** Symmetric companion: dropping the whole octahedral completion (`a` alone kept) and\ncomparing to the identity isolates `a` as the sole surviving first-order term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_single_restrict","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_single_restrict","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L56-L62","doc":"**First-order agreement, quantified.** If the three missing tetrahedra vanish (`b = c = d = 0`)\nthe four-factor holonomy collapses to the single dilog `1 + a` exactly — the single-`Φ_q` model is\nthe `b = c = d = 0` restriction of the octahedron, confirming `μ₀` is precisely the one-tetrahedron\ntruncation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_sub_single","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_sub_single","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L44-L54","doc":"**μ₀ is one of the four first-order terms.** The four-factor holonomy minus the single-dilog\ntruncation `(1 + a)` is exactly the *other three* first-order tetrahedron terms `(b + c + d)` plus\nall the higher-order (cross / Magnus) corrections. So `singleDilog a` captures `a` — one of four —\nat first order, and the remainder is the octahedral completion. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_expand","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog_expand","kind":"theorem","line":33,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L33-L42","doc":"**Exact expansion of the four-factor product** into elementary-symmetric orders. The\n**first-order (linear) part is `a + b + c + d`** — the four tetrahedra enter symmetrically; the\nquadratic `e₂`, cubic `e₃`, quartic `e₄` parts are the Magnus/cross (commutator-order) corrections. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.singleDilog","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.singleDilog","kind":"def","line":30,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L30-L31","doc":"The **single-dilogarithm (μ₀) truncation**: the QOU keeps one tetrahedron, `1 + a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.fourDilog","kind":"def","line":26,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/FourDilogFirstOrder.lean#L26-L28","doc":"The **octahedral holonomy** to leading order in each factor: the product of the four\ndilogarithm perturbations `(1 + aᵢ)`, one per ideal tetrahedron of the crossing's octahedron. ","signatureChunk":0}]}